---
/**
 * R16 — THE MASTHEAD. Round A, second half.
 *
 * The site has no navigation and did not have any before this slice.
 * Nothing renders over the Cover: her name is already enormous there,
 * and CONTEXT.md's Composition table gives the Cover no folio, no
 * kicker and no section numeral for the same reason. On scrolling PAST
 * the Cover a thin bar arrives carrying her name small plus a jump to
 * the Index, and withdraws on scrolling back. THE ARRIVAL IS THE
 * ANIMATION — there is nothing else moving in this file.
 *
 * ROUTE TAKEN: CSS SCROLL-DRIVEN ANIMATION. NO LISTENER, AND NO SECOND
 * LISTENER. CONTEXT.md's motion budget offers exactly two mechanisms
 * — "anything new either reuses the folio's listener or uses CSS
 * scroll-driven animation" — and names the second as preferred,
 * reserving the first for "where a listener is genuinely required."
 * It is not required here. Four reasons, in order of weight:
 *
 *   1. It adds ZERO JavaScript and does not open R12's shipped
 *      component. The folio's listener stays byte-identical.
 *   2. The reader's scroll IS the timing, so there is no duration, no
 *      distance and no easing constant to tune — the same property
 *      that made R17's scrub derivation-free.
 *   3. The arrival and the withdrawal are ONE range read in two
 *      directions, so the withdraw threshold cannot drift from the
 *      arrive threshold. A listener would need two comparisons and,
 *      to avoid chatter at the boundary, a hysteresis constant — a
 *      swept pixel this file has deleted four times.
 *   4. R17 already built the three-gate pattern this depends on. This
 *      is its second consumer, so the gates are precedent rather than
 *      invention.
 *
 * THE ENTRY-ANIMATION PATTERN, AND WHAT "FINISHED" MEANS HERE. The
 * pattern is "the finished state is the CSS default; JavaScript
 * applies the animated state." The masthead's CSS default is
 * `display: none` — the page exactly as R15 and R17 shipped it, with
 * no navigation. That is not a hidden resting state of the kind the
 * fade-up ban forbids, and the distinction is content:
 *
 *   THE BAR CARRIES NO CONTENT THAT IS NOT ALREADY ON THE PAGE. Her
 *   name is the Cover's <h1>. The Index is a section with its own
 *   <h2> and its own five entries. Nothing is lost when the bar does
 *   not render — a screenshot capture, a client-generated PDF and a
 *   failed script all get the finished page, which is the whole point
 *   of the pattern.
 *
 * The inverse would break the brief outright: a bar whose CSS default
 * is "present" renders over the Cover at scroll 0 with JavaScript off,
 * and "MUST NOT appear over the Cover at any scroll position" is not
 * conditional on a script running. Verified by rendered ink, not by
 * reading this comment — see the R16 report.
 *
 * NO LAYOUT SPACE, EVER. position: fixed, never sticky. Page height,
 * all ten section heights and all ten section tops are 0.000px against
 * the pre-R16 build at all thirteen widths.
 *
 * NO TRANSFORM TOUCHES A PHOTOGRAPH. R1.1: a transform on or above an
 * <img> promotes it to its own compositing layer and its
 * mix-blend-mode treatment silently stops blending. The only
 * transform R16 creates is on this element, which is a sibling of
 * <main> and contains no image. The three sections it borrows
 * timelines from — About, the Feature Quote and Contact — carry no
 * photograph at all per the Composition table, and view-timeline-name
 * is a declaration with no layout, paint or compositing effect in any
 * case. It is not a transform.
 *
 * NO NEW HEADING AND NO NEW LANDMARK. The ten-<h2> structure is
 * locked and is untouched. The name is aria-hidden — it is a running
 * head, a decorative repeat of the <h1> two screens up, and the folio
 * is aria-hidden for the same reason. The jump is a bare <a>, not a
 * <nav>: one link is not a navigation landmark, and adding one would
 * change a landmark structure R14 verified.
 *
 * COLLAPSE, ALL FOUR BANDS — stated here rather than retrofitted:
 *
 *   >=1280    band 1 : rendered
 *   1024-1279 band 2 : NOT rendered
 *   751-1023  band 3 : NOT rendered
 *   <=750     band 4 : NOT rendered
 *
 * BAND 1 ONLY, and the threshold is the folio's 1280 for the folio's
 * reason: "below 1280 the page has no motion at all, and that is
 * correct — a phone is a single stacked column of a printed object,
 * not a reduced version of a moving one." THIS IS NOT A MOBILE NAV.
 * It is not a nav at all below 1280; it does not exist there.
 *
 * ===================================================================
 * R19 — THE SECTION LIST. The INDEX link becomes a disclosure whose
 * panel carries every in-page destination.
 *
 * THE CONTENTS ARE src/data/sections.ts, IMPORTED RATHER THAN RETYPED.
 * CONTEXT.md's constraint 4: "sections.ts, in page order, minus the
 * Cover and minus the pivot," with the labels byte-identical to the
 * rendered <h2>s under the same Buffer.compare = 0 rule the heading
 * structure already enforces. Those two sections are exactly the two
 * that carry no entry in that file — verified on the live DOM before
 * this slice was written, all eight remaining comparisons 0 — so
 * iterating the file IS the constraint, and no hand-copied literal can
 * drift out of agreement with it. This is the file sections.ts was
 * created to stop being copied.
 *
 * THE LIST IS EIGHT ROWS, NOT NINE. CONTEXT.md's constraint 5 says R19
 * "multiplies one target into nine" and the brief says eight new
 * targets. Measured: ten sections, minus the Cover and minus the pivot,
 * is EIGHT — and one of those eight (#sec-index) is R16's existing
 * target, so seven are new. Both figures in the brief derive from the
 * same single off-by-one (ten minus the pivot, with the Cover subtracted
 * only once). The CONTENT rule is unambiguous and is what is built;
 * the count is reported rather than reconciled.
 *
 * NO NUMBERING, AND THE <ul> IS PART OF THAT. Constraint 4: the list
 * MUST NOT carry the Index's 01-05, because the page already runs three
 * numbering devices that MUST NOT be made to agree. An <ol> would make
 * the markup itself a fourth one — a screen reader announces "1 of 8"
 * from an <ol> whatever list-style says. The Index section uses <ol>
 * precisely because its numerals ARE its numbering; this list has none,
 * so it is a <ul> with role="list" (the role restores the semantics
 * Safari drops when list-style is none).
 *
 * THE TRIGGER READS "Contents", AND THAT IS THE ONE STRING THIS SLICE
 * AUTHORS. Constraint 4 puts the Index's own label in the list, under
 * the byte-identical rule, so the trigger cannot also read "Index"
 * without opening a panel whose sixth row repeats it — which reads as a
 * defect on sight whatever it measures. The constraint binds the
 * trigger's POSITION, not its string. "Contents" is the printed
 * object's word for a list of destinations, which is the register the
 * whole page is arguing for. Reversible in one word; flagged in the
 * report rather than slipped in.
 *
 * HOW THE PANEL INHERITS R16'S OBSIDIAN SUPPRESSION — THROUGH THE BAR,
 * NOT THROUGH A SECOND RULE. R16's suppression is not a selector. It is
 * the bar's own `transform: translateY(-100%)`, attached four times
 * across two keyframe rules, and the only thing that "goes quiet" is
 * the element carrying that transform. So the panel has to be INSIDE
 * THE BAR'S OWN BOX: the bar's height is intrinsic and the open panel
 * deepens it, so one translateY carries the name, the trigger and the
 * contents off the sheet together. There is no second rule to keep in
 * agreement, and nothing in R16's four attachments is edited.
 *
 * The alternative was an absolutely positioned panel, and it FAILS
 * measurably: a 280px panel hung below a 56px bar is at viewport y
 * 56-336, and the bar's withdrawal moves it to 0-280 — fully visible,
 * in Obsidian display type's way, on exactly the spreads R16 proved no
 * palette ink survives. Same family as R16's own background-attachment
 * finding: a transform governs its subtree and nothing outside it.
 *
 * WHAT THAT COSTS, STATED: while the panel is open the bar's travel is
 * its open height rather than 56px, over the same 56px of scroll. The
 * bar leaves faster than it arrived. That is the safe direction — more
 * suppression, not less — and it cannot happen at the Cover, where the
 * panel is closed by default and the bar is 56px exactly.
 *
 * ::details-content, NAMED BY R18 AND MET HERE. Chrome 148 resolves it
 * to `content-visibility: hidden` closed and `visible` open, display
 * block, position static. Two consequences this file depends on:
 *
 *   1. THE CLOSED PANEL CONTRIBUTES NO HEIGHT AND NO A11Y TREE. That
 *      is why the bar is 56px closed with no rule of its own saying so,
 *      and why the <nav> landmark exists only while the panel is open.
 *   2. IT CANNOT BE OPENED ON TRANSFORM AND OPACITY ALONE. The box's
 *      open/close IS content-visibility plus block-size; every recipe
 *      for animating it reaches for block-size or grid-template-rows,
 *      and CONTEXT.md allows transform and opacity only. So THE BOX IS
 *      NOT ANIMATED — reported rather than worked around, and no
 *      forbidden property is reached for. Animating the contents
 *      against a box that has already snapped would read as a loading
 *      state, not a reveal; R18.2 removed the Index row stagger on this
 *      page for an adjacent reason. The panel's only motion is the
 *      opacity on a pressed row, which is .idx-row's, verbatim.
 *
 * WHY THERE IS NOW A SCRIPT IN THIS FILE, AND EXACTLY WHAT IT IS FOR.
 * R16 carried none. Two of this slice's requirements cannot be
 * expressed in CSS on a <details> at all, and both were measured rather
 * than assumed — Chrome 148 leaves `open` true on Escape:
 *
 *   ESCAPE-TO-CLOSE WITH FOCUS RETURN. Required by CONTEXT.md's
 *   constraint 6. <details> has no Escape behaviour in any engine, and
 *   a disclosure whose content is hidden under the focused element
 *   drops focus to <body>.
 *
 *   CLOSE ON JUMP. Required BY constraint 5, transitively: every
 *   target's landing is measured against the bar at rest, and a panel
 *   left open occludes the top 280px of the section it just landed on.
 *   Outside-click was the alternative the brief offered and is NOT
 *   taken: it needs a document-level listener for a case Escape and the
 *   trigger's own toggle already cover.
 *
 * It is `is:inline`, so it is eight lines in index.html and zero bytes
 * in the bundle — the 226,474-byte budget is untouched. It adds no
 * scroll listener and no observer; R12's monopoly is intact. THE LIST
 * DOES NOT DEPEND ON IT: with JavaScript disabled the page has no bar
 * at all (R16's entry pattern, unchanged), and with the bar forced
 * visible and scripts off the disclosure still opens and closes, which
 * is the whole reason constraint 3 chose <details>. See the report.
 * ===================================================================
 */
import { sections } from '../data/sections';

/* MOBILE CONTENTS. The inline script below also serves Contents.astro's
 * .toc (below 1280): Escape and close-on-jump for both, and for .toc
 * alone a close on a tap outside. This disclosure's behaviour is
 * unchanged. .toc is mounted before this component so it exists when
 * the script runs. */

/* R21 SLICE 2 — THE LOCKUP SETS HER NAME IN THE BAR. Inlined exactly as
 * Cover.astro inlines it (PASS F): `?raw`, the <metadata> block and the
 * c2pa namespace stripped and nothing else, so the root keeps its own
 * viewBox, `fill="currentColor"` and `role="img"` byte-for-byte. The
 * wrapper carries aria-hidden, so the role="img" is never exposed. */
import lockupRaw from '../../brand_assets/mcclain-lockup.svg?raw';
const lockupSvg = lockupRaw
  .replace(/<metadata>[\s\S]*?<\/metadata>/, '')
  .replace(/\s+xmlns:c2pa="[^"]*"/, '');
---

{/* ACCESSIBILITY AUDIT. A <header> rather than a <div>: it is a sibling
    of <main>, so it exposes the banner landmark. Class-based styling is
    unchanged. */}
<header class="masthead">
  {/* R18.3. THE NAME LINKS TO THE TOP OF THE DOCUMENT.

      `#top` AND NOT `#sec-portrait`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS TWO THINGS.
      `#top` is the HTML spec's document-top fragment for the case where
      no element carries that id, so it lands at DOCUMENT top rather
      than at the Cover's box top; and it matches no `:target`, so
      R19's constraint-7 arrival rule never fires off it. Both halves
      are verified by measurement in the R18.3 report, not asserted.
      NOTHING carries id="top" and nothing is to be given it.

      THE ANCHOR WRAPS THE SPAN RATHER THAN REPLACING IT. R16 hid the
      name from the accessibility tree because it is already the
      Cover's <h1>, and that reasoning is untouched: the aria-hidden
      stays exactly where it was. The ARIA rule it could have broken —
      a focusable element INSIDE an aria-hidden subtree — is not
      engaged, because the hidden subtree is the span and the focusable
      element is its PARENT. This is the icon-button pattern, and the
      aria-label gives the link a functional name instead of repeating
      the heading. */}
  <a class="mast-home" href="#top" aria-label="Syreeta McClain, back to top">
    <span class="mast-name" aria-hidden="true">Dr. Syreeta McClain</span><span class="mast-mark" aria-hidden="true" set:html={lockupSvg}></span>
  </a>

  {/* ONE DISCLOSURE, SO "ONE THING OPEN AT A TIME" IS STRUCTURAL. No
      `name` attribute: the exclusive-accordion grouping it provides
      needs two or more <details> to mean anything, and a second
      disclosure in a 56px running head is not something this slice
      should make possible. */}
  <details class="mast-index">
    <summary class="mast-jump">Contents</summary>

    {/* THE LANDMARK IS INSIDE THE PANEL, NOT AROUND THE BAR. R16
        declined a <nav> on the stated ground that "one link is not a
        navigation landmark"; eight in-page destinations are one, so the
        reason has expired rather than been overruled. Placed inside
        ::details-content it inherits content-visibility: hidden, so the
        landmark — and R14's verified landmark structure — is unchanged
        while the panel is closed, and the navigation exists exactly
        while it is on screen. Verified against the rendered
        accessibility tree, not asserted. */}
    <nav class="mast-panel" aria-label="Sections">
      <ul class="mast-list" role="list">
        {sections.map((s) => (
          <li><a class="mast-row" href={`#${s.id}`}>{s.label}</a></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </nav>
  </details>
</header>

<script is:inline>
  /* R19. The two behaviours CSS cannot express on a <details>; see the
     note at the head of this file. Nothing here is required for the
     list to open, close or navigate. */
  (function () {
    document.querySelectorAll('.mast-index, .toc').forEach(function (d) {
      var s = d.querySelector('summary');
      d.addEventListener('keydown', function (e) {
        if (e.key === 'Escape' && d.open) { d.open = false; s.focus(); }
      });
      d.addEventListener('click', function (e) {
        if (e.target.closest('a')) d.open = false;
      });
    });
    var t = document.querySelector('.toc');
    if (t) document.addEventListener('pointerdown', function (e) {
      if (t.open && !t.contains(e.target)) t.open = false;
    });
  })();
</script>

<style>
  /* ================================================================
     THE RESTING STATE IS NOT RENDERED.

     This declaration is the entry-animation pattern's whole no-JS
     answer, and it is deliberately the FIRST thing in this file.
     Everything below is inside three gates and is ADDED on top of it;
     nothing anywhere subtracts from a visible default.
     ================================================================ */
  .masthead{ display: none; }

  /* ---- THE TYPE --------------------------------------------------
     The bar is a miniature of the page's own pairing: Fraunces for
     the name, Montserrat for the label — a lede beside a kicker, at
     one twentieth of the size. That is what keeps it from reading as
     site chrome bolted onto an editorial spread.

     THE NAME IS SET AT --mast-name-size, WHICH IS --lede-size TAKEN
     ONE FULL R7 STEP DOWNWARD (0.4872-0.5208 x a lede across band 1).
     The derivation is written above the token in global.css. In the
     bar it is 26.66-32.00px against a lede's 52-64px: one whole tier
     apart, on the page's own ladder, at every width — which is the
     answer to "MUST NOT compete with any lede."

     ALL FOUR FRAUNCES AXES ARE STATED. Fraunces defaults to wght 900,
     WONK on and SOFT 100 when unspecified, and a rule that leaves an
     axis out ships a decision nobody made. This tier is not in the
     optical table's five rows, so it is placed against the table's
     own logic rather than by borrowing a row that means something
     else: at 27px the name is READ AT A GLANCE, not SEEN at display
     scale, so it takes the legibility end of the axis — opsz 24 and
     SOFT 60 are the sharp end of the table's "body-adjacent / small"
     band (opsz 9-24, SOFT 60-80), which is where 27px belongs. wght
     400 rather than the display tiers' 340: a 27px face needs the
     weight the 96px faces can do without. WONK 0, as everywhere on
     the page except the pivot's italic.

     line-height: var(--mast-h) IS AN OPTICAL CENTRING, SOLVED FROM
     THE FACE, NOT A NUDGE. Setting the line box to the bar's own
     height puts the half-leading either side of the ink, so the
     name's cap-to-baseline block centres itself: measured against the
     R9.6 metrics it lands 0.30px off the bar's centre at 1440 and
     0.36px off at 1920. There is no magic offset here to go stale,
     and R6.3's "an em constant cannot track a rounded font metric"
     does not bite — nothing is being modelled, the line box is doing
     it. */
  .mast-name{
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 24, 'wght' 400, 'SOFT' 60, 'WONK' 0;
    font-size: var(--mast-name-size);
    line-height: var(--mast-h);
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    color: var(--obsidian);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* ---- R18.3. THE NAME'S ANCHOR ------------------------------------
     `display: flex` IS THE WHOLE REASON THE NAME DOES NOT MOVE, and it
     is not a style choice. The span was a direct flex item of .masthead
     and was therefore BLOCKIFIED; the anchor now takes that slot. If
     the anchor were left inline it would generate a line box of its
     own, and that box's height would be set by the anchor's STRUT —
     .masthead's inherited font and line-height, not the name's — so
     the 26.66px ink would be re-seated against a strut it has nothing
     to do with. As a flex container the anchor establishes no line box
     at all: the span is blockified exactly as before, at offset 0 in a
     parent with no padding, border or margin. Measured 0.000px on the
     name's rendered INK at 1920, 1440 and 1280, with the ink taken
     from a Range over the text node rather than from the element box,
     because an element box can change shape while the ink stays put
     and only the ink is the thing that must not move.

     COLOUR AND UNDERLINE ARE NEUTRALISED EXPLICITLY. The UA sheet
     gives an anchor its own colour and a text-decoration, and either
     one would be a visible change to a name that R18.1's Legacy scrub
     is seated against by construction. `color: inherit` rather than a
     second copy of var(--obsidian): .mast-name already declares its
     own colour, so this only stops the UA value from existing.

     OPACITY, AND WHY NOT THE BAR'S OTHER TWO TREATMENTS. This is
     .edu-link's hover minus its translate — the page's own link
     primitive, per CONTEXT.md's R18.3 ruling. It is NOT
     summary.mast-jump's, which is not a link and carries no hover by
     construction under R19's constraint 6; and it is NOT .mast-row's,
     which is a 10.5px sans panel row against a 26.66px Fraunces name.
     Opacity is scale-free and survives that tier gap; an underline
     would not.

     transition-property NAMES opacity ONLY. Never transition-all. */
  .mast-home{
    display: flex;
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: opacity 260ms var(--ease);
  }

  .mast-home:hover{ opacity: 0.7; }
  .mast-home:active{ opacity: 0.55; }

  /* R16'S RING, AT ITS OWN FOUR PIXELS OF OFFSET. The bar carries two
     focus treatments and they agree on the ring — 1px solid Obsidian —
     differing only in offset, where .mast-row's two is a documented
     deviation forced by the panel's 28px lattice ("THE RING IS R16'S,
     AT TWO PIXELS OF OFFSET RATHER THAN FOUR"). The name sits on no
     lattice, so it takes the unmodified original, which is also
     byte-identical to .edu-link's. No text-decoration-color: the name
     carries no underline for one to apply to. */
  .mast-home:focus-visible{
    outline: 1px solid var(--obsidian);
    outline-offset: 4px;
  }

  /* ---- THE JUMP --------------------------------------------------
     The kicker register, verbatim: Montserrat 400 at 10.5px on 0.26em,
     uppercase, line-height on the baseline lattice. It is the page's
     established small-label voice and the bar has no business
     inventing a second one.

     OBSIDIAN, NOT THE KICKER'S SLATE. A kicker is a label and this is
     the page's only link that is not inside a section — it is the one
     interactive object in the bar, and the page's own link primitive
     (.edu-link) is Obsidian. The underline is .edu-link's too,
     rgba(71, 68, 64, 0.42) at a 4px offset: a non-colour affordance,
     per 1.4.1, and the offset rather than a border so nothing is
     struck through.

     NO HOVER TREATMENT, BY INSTRUCTION. The 21st.dev letter-roll is
     not ruled in and this round builds no hover of any kind — not on
     the name, not on the link. focus-visible is NOT a hover treatment
     and is built: a link with no focus indicator fails WCAG 2.1 SC
     2.4.7 outright, and the ring below is the page's own, measured on
     Porcelain in R10 and re-derived on Obsidian in R14. See the R16
     report for the one-line hover this bar should probably have and
     deliberately does not. */
  .mast-jump{
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 10.5px;
    line-height: var(--baseline);
    letter-spacing: 0.26em;
    /* half the tracking, so the trailing letter-space does not push
       the label off the page's right trim — the same compensation
       .kicker makes on the left. */
    margin-right: -0.26em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--obsidian);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
    text-underline-offset: 4px;
    text-decoration-color: rgba(71, 68, 64, 0.42);
  }

  .mast-jump:focus-visible{
    outline: 1px solid var(--obsidian);
    outline-offset: 4px;
    text-decoration-color: var(--obsidian);
  }

  /* ---- R19. THE DISCLOSURE -----------------------------------------
     THE <details> TAKES THE REMAINING WIDTH AND THE <summary> SEATS
     ITSELF AT THE RIGHT TRIM INSIDE IT. The trigger cannot be the flex
     item any more, because the flex item is now the thing that owns the
     panel; so the item stretches and the trigger right-aligns inside it
     with an auto margin. The rendered x of the label is unchanged —
     `margin-right: -0.26em` resolves the same against a block's
     over-constrained margin equation as it did against a flex end, and
     it is measured, not reasoned: 0.000px at all three band-1 widths.

     min-width: 0 because a flex item's automatic minimum size is its
     content's min-content width, and the panel's longest label would
     otherwise be able to push the bar wider than the viewport. */
  .mast-index{
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* The trigger. EVERY TYPE DECLARATION IS R16'S, UNTOUCHED — the rule
     above still sets all of it, and this adds only the box. The element
     changed from <a> to <summary> and the register did not.

     height: var(--mast-h) KEEPS THE NAME ROW AT TWO BASELINES. It is
     the row, not the bar, that is 56px now, and this is the
     declaration that says so. The label's own 28px line box centres in
     it, which is the same 14px offset `align-items: center` produced on
     a 56px bar — identical, and measured as such.

     THE MARKER COMES OFF IN BOTH ENGINES, exactly as summary.idx-row
     already does it in Index.astro. `display: flex` alone would drop
     the ::marker by taking the box out of `list-item`, but the
     -webkit- pseudo needs saying for older WebKit and list-style: none
     for anything that honours neither. Three lines, one behaviour,
     copied from the precedent in this build rather than invented. */
  summary.mast-jump{
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: flex-end;
    height: var(--mast-h);
    width: fit-content;
    margin-inline-start: auto;
    list-style: none;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  summary.mast-jump::-webkit-details-marker{
    display: none;
  }

  /* THE OPEN STATE IS THE UNDERLINE GOING OBSIDIAN — R16's own
     focus-visible treatment, reused rather than a second affordance
     invented. NOT A HOVER TREATMENT: CONTEXT.md's constraint 6 permits
     none on the trigger beyond a recommendation that is not ruled in,
     and this is a disclosure's expanded state, which a control that
     opens something is required to show. The native `open` attribute
     carries it to the accessibility tree; this carries it to the eye. */
  .mast-index[open] > .mast-jump{
    text-decoration-color: var(--obsidian);
  }

  /* ---- R19. THE PANEL ----------------------------------------------
     IT HAS NO GROUND, NO BORDER, NO RADIUS AND NO SHADOW, AND THAT IS
     THE WHOLE DESIGN ARGUMENT. The bar already paints the page's own
     --page-ground sized to the viewport, so a deeper bar paints the
     identical pixels the body would have painted in that strip — the
     band stays invisible over the seven Porcelain spreads and what
     prints is type in the sheet's top margin. A panel with an edge of
     its own would be a dropdown on a printed spread. THE SHEET'S HEAD
     SIMPLY OPENS: two baselines closed, twelve open.

     padding-block IS ONE BASELINE TOP AND BOTTOM, so the open bar is
     2 + 1 + 8 + 1 = 12 x --baseline = 336px exactly, and every edge in
     it lands on the page's own vertical unit. Nothing here is a chosen
     pixel; the only quantity is the page's lattice, used four times.

     R16's trim hairline is UNTOUCHED and needs no edit: it is an
     ::after at bottom: 0 of the bar, so it rules off the head wherever
     the head now ends. One hairline, not two — the page's hairline-rule
     contexts are a counted quantity and this slice adds none. */
  .mast-panel{
    padding-block: var(--baseline);
  }

  /* ONE COLUMN OF EIGHT, FLUSH RIGHT, ENDING ON THE RIGHT TRIM.

     TWO COLUMNS OF FOUR WERE BUILT FIRST AND WERE REJECTED ON SIGHT,
     WHICH IS THE ONLY WAY THAT DEFECT COULD HAVE BEEN CAUGHT — it
     measures correctly at every width. Two flush-right columns put
     column one's right edge 24px from column two's ragged left edge,
     so every visual line reads as a run-on: "Premier Leadership
     Charging It to the Game" is one line to the eye, not two entries.
     Widening the gap to the page trim's 64px reduced it and did not
     remove it — the reader still has to work out that it is two
     columns. One column has no such state. Captures of all three are
     in the report.

     WHAT IT COSTS, STATED PLAINLY: the open head is 336px rather than
     224px — 12 x --baseline instead of 8, about a third of a 1080px
     viewport. Bought deliberately. The panel is a transient state that
     closes on the jump it exists to perform, and the emptiness to the
     left of the list is the sheet's top margin, which is this page's
     own argument rather than a hole in it.

     WHY FLUSH RIGHT AND NOT FLUSH LEFT. R16's claim is that the bar
     "introduces NO fourth edge: the edge it uses is one of the three
     that already exist." A flush-left list would introduce a new left
     edge inside the head and break that claim — and a left edge one
     column off an existing one is the failure CONTEXT.md's
     Three-left-edges rule names by name. Flush right keeps the bar's
     only left edge at --gutter, where the name is, and hangs the list
     from the right trim the trigger already sits on, so the panel reads
     as belonging to the control that opened it. Measured: the rows'
     right edge and the trigger's rendered ink share one x at all three
     band-1 widths.

     grid-auto-rows: var(--baseline) LOCKS THE PITCH TO THE PAGE'S
     LATTICE rather than letting it fall out of the type's line box, so
     the panel's depth stays a count of baselines if the row size ever
     moves. DOM order, visual order and tab order are one sequence;
     there is no positive tabindex and nothing kept in agreement by
     hand. */
  .mast-list{
    display: grid;
    grid-auto-rows: var(--baseline);
    justify-items: end;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* THE ROWS. Fraunces, at --mast-row-size — the bar's name taken one
     HALF R7 step down, on R15's own measured constant. The derivation
     is written above the token in global.css. 18.61-22.33px across band
     1, against the name's 26.66-32.00px: subordinate to it by an
     unmistakable amount, and still the largest thing in the panel.

     FRAUNCES, NOT THE SANS, AND THAT IS THE CALL THIS PANEL LIVES OR
     DIES ON. Eight rows of tracked uppercase Montserrat is a site
     navigation and would announce itself as one; the page's argument is
     a printed spread. The Index section's own entry names are Fraunces
     and this list is that section's function moved into the running
     head, so the two devices speak with one voice. The bar now carries
     three registers and they are the page's three: a lede (the name),
     a contents entry (the rows), a kicker (the trigger).

     ALL FOUR AXES STATED, per the rule that a Fraunces rule leaving an
     axis out ships a decision nobody made. wght 400, SOFT 60, WONK 0
     are the name's, verbatim — one voice, two sizes. opsz is the one
     that moves: 18 rather than 24, because opsz tracks the rendered
     tier and 18.6-22.3px is lower in the table's body-adjacent band
     (opsz 9-24, SOFT 60-80) than the name's 27-32px. Same band, one
     notch sharper.

     line-height: var(--baseline) puts the rows on the page's lattice at
     1.25-1.50 — and the row pitch IS the baseline, so the panel's depth
     is a count of baselines rather than a measured block.

     NO UNDERLINE AT REST, UNDERLINE ON HOVER. This is .idx-row's
     vocabulary exactly (Index.astro), which is the page's own answer
     for a list whose every row is a link: the list context carries the
     affordance, and eight resting underlines in a 336px head would read
     as a sitemap. SC 1.4.1 is about links inside a block of text, which
     this is not. Carried over verbatim rather than re-derived, so the
     two lists cannot drift. */
  .mast-row{
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 18, 'wght' 400, 'SOFT' 60, 'WONK' 0;
    font-size: var(--mast-row-size);
    line-height: var(--baseline);
    color: var(--obsidian);
    text-decoration: none;
    /* Only opacity transitions here, as in the Index. Never
       transition-all, and never a colour transition. */
    transition: opacity 140ms var(--ease);
  }

  /* HOVER AND FOCUS TAKE THE SAME RESPONSE, which is R13.1's finding on
     the Index rows: keyboard focus earns what the mouse earns. */
  .mast-row:hover,
  .mast-row:focus-visible{
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
    text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
  }

  /* THE RING IS R16'S, AT TWO PIXELS OF OFFSET RATHER THAN FOUR, AND
     THE TWO IS MEASURED — INCLUDING THE WIDTH WHERE THE SLACK RUNS OUT.
     The rows sit on the lattice, so the row box IS the 28px pitch and
     there is no gap between one row's box and the next. What varies is
     the rendered text box inside it:

         1280   18.90px type   text box 23px   half-leading 2.50px
         1440   18.61px type   text box 23px   half-leading 2.50px
         1920   22.33px type   text box 28px   half-leading 0.00px

     So at 1280 and 1440 a 2px offset lands inside the half-leading and
     clears the next row's text box; AT 1920 THERE IS NO HALF-LEADING AND
     ANY POSITIVE OFFSET CROSSES INTO IT. That is not a defect and it is
     written down rather than hidden: only ONE row is ever focused, so
     two rings are never drawn at once, and the ring's own contrast is
     measured against the ground it actually sits on (13.55-13.85:1, see
     the report) rather than against a neighbour. Four pixels would have
     put the ring through the adjacent row's INK at every width; two puts
     it through empty leading at two widths and through the neighbour's
     box but not its glyphs at the third.

     NOTHING CLIPS IT. Verified rather than assumed — every ancestor from
     the row up to <body> carries overflow: visible and clip-path: none —
     because R13.1 shipped a clip that had to be released for exactly
     this reason. */
  .mast-row:focus-visible{
    outline: 1px solid var(--obsidian);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  .mast-row:active{
    opacity: 0.7;
  }

  /* ================================================================
     THE THREE GATES, AND THE ONE MECHANISM.

     Identical to R17's, deliberately — the masthead is the second
     consumer of a pattern that already shipped, not a second pattern:

       html.js-motion   BaseLayout adds this in <head>, inline and
                        synchronous. No JavaScript, no class, no rule
                        in this block matches, and the page renders
                        exactly as R15 and R17 shipped it.
       @supports        an engine without scroll-driven animation
                        never parses the block, rather than running
                        the keyframes off the document timeline and
                        sliding a bar in on a stopwatch.
       min-width 1280   band 1 only. The folio's threshold, for the
                        folio's reason.

     prefers-reduced-motion is the fourth and is written INTO the
     media query rather than left to global.css's blanket
     `animation-duration: 0.001ms !important`, because duration is not
     what drives a scroll-driven animation and that override does not
     reliably stop one (R17). Under `reduce` the bar does not render
     at all — which is the right answer and not a compromise: the
     arrival IS the masthead, the page had no navigation before this
     slice, and the Index section still does the navigating.
     ================================================================ */
  /* ---- ACCESSIBILITY AUDIT: THE KEYBOARD BAR (2.4.7, 2.4.11) ------
     The bar used to render only behind all four gates above. With
     reduced motion, or in an engine without scroll-driven animation, it
     did not exist, and with motion on its two controls took focus while
     the bar was parked off the trim at the top of the page.

     So from 1280 up the bar is ALWAYS laid out, under every motion
     preference, and parked off the trim by the same translateY(-100%)
     the arrival animates from. Parked, it is exactly as invisible to a
     pointer as it was when it did not render. It comes down only while
     one of its own controls has KEYBOARD focus:

       :has(:focus-visible), not :focus-within. A click on Contents also
       focuses it, and :focus-within would hold the bar down after mouse
       use, which would change the bar for pointer and touch readers.

     !important is the mechanism, not a shortcut: the motion block below
     drives `transform` from a scroll-driven animation, and an animated
     value outranks every normal declaration. An important one outranks
     the animation, and only this one rule carries it.

     The declarations repeat the motion block's layout byte for byte;
     that block still owns the arrival, the withdrawals and the hairline
     there, and nothing in it is changed. Placed BEFORE it, so at 1600
     the motion block's own cap padding still wins where it applies. */
  @media (min-width: 1280px){
    :global(html.js-motion) .masthead{
      display: flex;
      align-items: flex-start;
      position: fixed;
      inset-block-start: 0;
      inset-inline: 0;
      z-index: 50;
      padding-inline: var(--gutter);
      background-color: var(--porcelain);
      background-image: var(--page-ground);
      background-repeat: no-repeat;
      background-position: left top;
      background-size: 100% 100vh;
      transform: translateY(-100%);
    }
    :global(html.js-motion) .masthead:has(:focus-visible){
      transform: none !important;
    }
    :global(html.js-motion) .masthead::after{
      content: '';
      position: absolute;
      inset-inline: 0;
      bottom: 0;
      height: 1px;
      background: var(--hair);
    }
    :global(html.js-motion) .mast-name{
      position: absolute;
      width: 1px;
      height: 1px;
      padding: 0;
      margin: -1px;
      overflow: hidden;
      clip-path: inset(50%);
      white-space: nowrap;
      border: 0;
    }
    :global(html.js-motion) .mast-mark{
      --mast-mark-h: calc(var(--mast-h) - 2 * var(--s1));
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      height: var(--mast-h);
      margin-left: calc(var(--mast-mark-h) * 3.5692 * -0.0040839);
    }
    :global(html.js-motion) .mast-mark :global(svg){
      display: block;
      height: var(--mast-mark-h);
      width: auto;
      max-width: none;
    }
  }
  @media (min-width: 1600px){
    :global(html.js-motion) .masthead{
      padding-inline: max(var(--gutter), calc((100% - var(--measure-cap)) / 2));
    }
  }

  @media (min-width: 1280px) and (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference){
    @supports (animation-timeline: view()){

      /* ---- THE TIMELINES ------------------------------------------
         Declared HERE, on other components' sections, and that is
         deliberate. The masthead owns its own mechanism; About, the
         Feature Quote and Contact know nothing about it and none of
         those three files is opened by this slice. A view-timeline
         name has no layout, paint or compositing effect — verified
         0.000px on every section at all thirteen widths.

         timeline-scope hoists the names to :root because the bar is a
         sibling of <main>, not a descendant of any section, and
         timeline-name lookup walks ancestors only. --pivot is R17's
         OWN timeline on the same element, consumed rather than
         duplicated: a second view-timeline-name declaration on .quote
         would replace R17's rather than join it, and this file is not
         allowed to move R17's finished state. Verified: the pivot's
         scrub start-scale is unchanged, 0.487179 at 1440, with the
         name hoisted. */
      :global(html.js-motion){
        timeline-scope: --page-turn, --pivot, --close;
      }
      :global(html.js-motion) :global(.about){
        view-timeline-name: --page-turn;
        view-timeline-axis: block;
      }
      :global(html.js-motion) :global(#sec-contact){
        view-timeline-name: --close;
        view-timeline-axis: block;
      }
      /* NO scroll-margin-top ON THE JUMP'S TARGET, AND THAT IS THE
         MEASURED ANSWER, NOT AN OMISSION.

         `scroll-margin-top: var(--mast-h)` is the reflex here and it
         was built first. It is wrong, and only a measurement shows
         why: it lands the reader 56px SHORT of the Index's top, which
         is 56px back inside the Feature Quote — and the Feature Quote
         is an Obsidian spread, where this bar is quiet by rule. So
         clicking the masthead's own link made the masthead disappear.
         Measured: scrollY 5774 at 1440, bar bottom at viewport 0.00.
         Self-consistent, and it reads as a bug.

         With no scroll margin the target lands flush: the Index's top
         trim on the viewport's top trim, at the exact scroll position
         where the pivot's return completes, so the bar is present and
         at rest. Nothing is occluded either — the Index's own top
         padding is 110px against the bar's 56, leaving 54px of
         clearance before the section's first ink. THAT DEPENDENCY IS
         REAL AND IS WRITTEN DOWN HERE: if the Index's top padding ever
         falls below --mast-h, this needs a scroll margin again, and
         the landing scroll needs re-checking against the pivot's
         withdrawal range at the same time. */

      /* ---- THE BAR ------------------------------------------------
         FIXED, NEVER STICKY. Sticky reserves layout space and would
         change the page's height and every section top below it.

         IT PAINTS THE PAGE'S OWN GROUND. --page-ground plus
         background-attachment: fixed, which positions a background
         against the viewport rather than the element's box — so the
         bar paints the identical pixels the body would have painted
         in that 56px strip, and over the seven Porcelain spreads the
         BAND IS INVISIBLE. What prints is her name, the label and one
         hairline, sitting in the sheet's top margin. That is the
         whole design: a running head, not a panel.

         IT HAS TO PAINT SOMETHING. A groundless fixed bar lets display
         type scroll straight through a 27px name. Occlusion is not
         decoration here, it is the reason the bar is legible at all —
         and painting the page ground is the only fill that costs the
         composition nothing. CONTEXT.md: "drama comes from type scale
         and negative space, never from colour volume."

         z-index 50 sits above the folio's 40 and below the grain
         sheet's 60, so the page's grain runs over the masthead
         exactly as it runs over everything else. The bar is part of
         the printed sheet, not a layer floating above it.

         NO box-shadow. R14 measured "zero box-shadow on the page,
         still" and this slice keeps that true — the hairline is an
         ::after, which draws no box and contributes no layout, rather
         than a border, which would eat 1px out of the derived 56px. */
      :global(html.js-motion) .masthead{
        /* R19. THREE CHANGES, AND ALL THREE ARE FORCED BY "THE PANEL
           INHERITS THE SUPPRESSION THROUGH THE BAR."

           `align-items: flex-start`, not `center`. With the panel in
           flow the bar is 336px tall while open, and `center` would
           drop the name and the trigger to the middle of THAT box.
           Flush-start is identical when closed and nowhere else: the
           name's line box is --mast-h (R16's optical centring, below)
           and the trigger's box is --mast-h too, so in a 56px bar
           `center` and `flex-start` put both at y 0. Measured 0.000px
           on the name's and the trigger's rendered boxes.

           NO `height`, AND THAT IS THE MECHANISM. The bar's height is
           now intrinsic: the trigger's --mast-h row closed, that row
           plus the panel open. A declared height would leave the panel
           overflowing a 56px box — visible, but OUTSIDE the box the
           withdrawal's `translateY(-100%)` is measured against, which
           is precisely the Obsidian failure this slice must not ship.
           Closed, the intrinsic height is 56.000px, measured at all
           three band-1 widths.

           NO `justify-content`. There are two flex items and the second
           one now owns the panel, so it takes the remaining width
           (`flex: 1`, below) and seats its own trigger at the right
           trim with an auto margin. `space-between` on a stretched
           second item does nothing, so it is removed rather than left
           to read as live. */
        display: flex;
        align-items: flex-start;
        position: fixed;
        inset-block-start: 0;
        inset-inline: 0;
        z-index: 50;
        /* The page trim. The name's left edge lands on --gutter,
           which is the grid's column 1 — the kicker's edge, the
           outermost edge the page already has. The bar introduces NO
           fourth edge: it is not inside a section, and the edge it
           uses is one of the three that already exist. */
        padding-inline: var(--gutter);

        /* ---- THE GROUND, AND WHY IT IS NOT background-attachment ----
           MEASURED FAILURE, FIXED. This first shipped as
           `background-attachment: fixed`, which positions a background
           against the viewport and should have made the bar paint the
           identical pixels the body paints in that strip. It does not,
           because THIS ELEMENT CARRIES A TRANSFORM: a transformed
           element is a containing block for fixed descendants, and
           Chrome degrades `background-attachment: fixed` to `scroll`
           inside one. The positioning area silently became the bar's
           own 1440 x 56 box, so the white radial's 78% radius resolved
           against 56px instead of 900px and the wash fell out of the
           bar over its own height. Measured in the right gutter, where
           no ink exists at any scroll position: the bar's ground ran up
           to 16/255 darker than the page's at the bar's foot, and
           agreed only at its very top row. Not visible in a thumbnail,
           very visible as a band once you know to look.

           This is R1.1's lesson arriving through paint instead of
           blending: a transform on the element itself silently breaks a
           mechanism that is declared somewhere else entirely.

           THE FIX DECLARES THE POSITIONING AREA INSTEAD OF INHERITING
           IT. The bar is fixed at the viewport's top-left, so sizing
           the background to the viewport and pinning it to the box's
           origin reproduces exactly what the body resolves the gradient
           against — with no dependence on background-attachment at all,
           and therefore nothing for a transform to take away. Width is
           `100%`, the bar's own width, not `100vw`: the fixed
           containing block excludes a classic scrollbar and `100vw`
           does not. Re-measured: 0 of 255 on every channel. */
        background-color: var(--porcelain);
        background-image: var(--page-ground);
        background-repeat: no-repeat;
        background-position: left top;
        background-size: 100% 100vh;

        /* THE RESTING TRANSFORM, held over the Cover. Also the
           animation's own `from`, so the two cannot disagree. */
        transform: translateY(-100%);

        /* ---- THE FOUR ATTACHMENTS ----------------------------------
           ONE PROPERTY — translateY — AND TWO KEYFRAME RULES, ATTACHED
           FOUR TIMES. Later in the list wins where two are live, which
           is what lets a bare 0%/100% pair express four boundaries
           with nothing swept.

           LONGHANDS, NEVER THE `animation` SHORTHAND. R17 records this
           as a gotcha and it is the second hit: the shorthand resets
           animation-timeline to `auto` and animation-range to
           `normal`, so above them it silently detaches the animation
           from its timeline and below them it silently discards both.
           It also sets animation-duration, which must stay `auto` for
           a scroll-driven animation to fill its range.

           1. ARRIVE, on About's view timeline.
              `exit 0%` is the instant About's top edge reaches the
              viewport's top edge — which is the instant the Cover's
              LAST PIXEL leaves it, because the Cover's bottom and
              About's top are the same coordinate (measured: 720.000
              and 720.000 at every band-1 width). So the bar's travel
              cannot begin until the Cover is gone. Measured at 1440:
              the bar's own bottom edge is at viewport y = 0.00 at
              scroll 720 — ZERO RENDERED PIXELS over the Cover, at the
              last scroll position the Cover occupies.

              The travel is --mast-h and it runs over --mast-h OF
              SCROLL. One quantity, used twice: the bar descends at
              exactly the rate the reader scrolls, which is the only
              rate the page already has. There is no duration and no
              easing curve, and there is nothing to tune.

              fill-mode BOTH: backwards fill holds the `from` over the
              whole Cover, forwards fill holds the finished state for
              the rest of the page. A view timeline stays active after
              its subject has left the scrollport — measured to the
              document's last scrollable pixel.

           2 and 3. WITHDRAW AND RETURN, on the Feature Quote's.
           4.      WITHDRAW, on Contact's.
              THE BAR DOES NOT PRINT ON THE OBSIDIAN SPREADS. This is
              forced by measurement, not chosen — see the R16 report's
              contrast table. The short version: the locked palette has
              five colours and NONE of them clears 3:1 against both
              Porcelain and Obsidian; the best any single colour could
              do against both grounds is 3.72:1, and no palette colour
              is near it. A bar that prints on both grounds is
              therefore illegible on one of them, and colour is not an
              animatable property here — "animate transform and
              opacity only."

              So the bar does on the dark spreads what the folio
              already does over the pivot and what CONTEXT.md's own
              Route 3 recommends for the folio over Contact: it goes
              quiet. The rule is "print on the Porcelain spreads, quiet
              on the Obsidian ones," which is a rule rather than an
              exception. It withdraws by the same gesture and the same
              --mast-h of travel as the arrival — one move, in both
              directions, at all four boundaries.

              fill-mode FORWARDS on 2, 3 and 4, never `both`: a
              backwards fill on any of them would hold its `from`
              across the whole page above its own range and hide the
              bar everywhere. Forwards-only contributes nothing before
              its range, which is what lets the four compose. */
        animation-name: mast-arrive, mast-withdraw, mast-arrive, mast-withdraw;
        animation-timing-function: linear;
        animation-fill-mode: both, forwards, forwards, forwards;
        animation-timeline: --page-turn, --pivot, --pivot, --close;
        animation-range:
          exit 0% exit var(--mast-h),
          exit 0% exit var(--mast-h),
          exit calc(100% - var(--mast-h)) exit 100%,
          exit 0% exit var(--mast-h);
      }

      /* The trim rule. Smoked Slate's hairline family (--hair is
         Obsidian at 0.16 and is the page's light-ground hairline
         token), NOT gold: the gold budget is 3 of 3 saturated accents
         and 0 of 8 hairline contexts, and R16 spends nothing from
         either. Absolutely positioned so it draws no box and takes no
         part in the derived 56px. */
      :global(html.js-motion) .masthead::after{
        content: '';
        position: absolute;
        inset-inline: 0;
        bottom: 0;
        height: 1px;
        background: var(--hair);
      }

      /* FROM off the trim, TO the CSS default — and back. The finished
         states are `none` rather than `translateY(0)` so that at rest
         the bar carries no transform at all. */
      @keyframes mast-arrive{
        from{ transform: translateY(-100%); }
        to  { transform: none; }
      }
      @keyframes mast-withdraw{
        from{ transform: none; }
        to  { transform: translateY(-100%); }
      }
    }
  }

  /* R20 -- THE CAP. The bar's whole point is that it introduces no
     fourth edge: the name lands on --gutter, which is the grid's column
     1. Above 1600 column 1 moves inward, so the bar's padding has to
     follow it or the name is left alone out on the old trim while every
     section's type sits centred hundreds of pixels to its right.

     100%, like .sheet: the bar is position: fixed, so its percentage
     resolves against the initial containing block, which excludes a
     classic scrollbar. Exact, and no vw needed -- nothing here bleeds.

     THE SELECTOR CHAIN IS COPIED, NOT SIMPLIFIED. The bar's padding is
     declared at :global(html.js-motion) .masthead inside BOTH a media
     query and an @supports -- the bar does not exist at all outside
     that combination, since .masthead is display:none by default. A
     flat `.masthead` rule here loses the specificity contest silently
     and measures 64px at every width, which is exactly what the first
     build of this rule did. min-width:1600 already implies the 1280
     the original carries. */
  @media (min-width: 1600px) and (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference){
    @supports (animation-timeline: view()){
      :global(html.js-motion) .masthead{
        padding-inline: max(var(--gutter), calc((100% - var(--measure-cap)) / 2));
      }
    }
  }

  /* R21 SLICE 2 -- THE LOCKUP REPLACES THE NAME'S INK, JACKSON'S RULING.
     PASS E.1 measured this artwork needing 161-210px of height for its
     thinnest stroke to survive 1.25 device px at dsf 1. The bar is 56px.
     Ruled to ship anyway: the script "dr" and the hairline serifs thin
     out and the mark reads as "SM". A known, accepted cost -- see the
     PASS E.1 amendment in CONTEXT.md -- not a regression.

     HEIGHT IS DERIVED FROM THE BAR'S INNER HEIGHT, --mast-h (the bar
     carries no vertical padding): the ink is --mast-h - 2 x --s1, see
     the R21.4 correction at the rule.
     Width follows from the viewBox (3.5692:1), never set. The box is hung
     left by the artwork's own left slack (0.40839% of its width, the same
     figure .cover-mark uses) so the INK lands on --gutter, the edge the
     name's ink used.

     THE TEXT STAYS AND IS VISUALLY HIDDEN, same clip-path mechanism as
     .cover-name-text. It keeps its own aria-hidden (R16: it is already the
     page's <h1>); the link's accessible name is still aria-label="Back to
     top". The mark wrapper is aria-hidden, so no image is exposed.

     COLOUR IS INHERITED. Nothing here sets color or fill on the mark;
     fill="currentColor" resolves through .mast-home's `color: inherit` to
     the bar's Obsidian.

     THE SELECTOR CHAIN IS COPIED FROM THE BAR'S OWN RULES, not flattened.
     The bar exists only as :global(html.js-motion) .masthead inside this
     media query and this @supports; R20 shipped a flat rule that lost to
     that chain silently. Band 1 only, because the bar is band 1 only --
     below 1280 there is no bar and nothing to replace.
     ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
  .mast-mark{ display: none; }
  @media (min-width: 1280px) and (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference){
    @supports (animation-timeline: view()){
      :global(html.js-motion) .mast-name{
        position: absolute;
        width: 1px;
        height: 1px;
        padding: 0;
        margin: -1px;
        overflow: hidden;
        clip-path: inset(50%);
        white-space: nowrap;
        border: 0;
      }
      /* R21.4 CORRECTION. Fitted at the full --mast-h the INK filled
